column30:
In 2020, Essex County Council (ECC) established the Essex Climate Action Commission (ECAC) as an independent advisory body to support Essex on its journey to tackle climate change, reduce greenhouse gas emissions and to build a climate resilient county. The ECAC has over 30 members. They include local councillors, academics, business people and two members of the Young Essex Assembly. In July 2021, the ECAC published its report, 'Net Zero: Making Essex Carbon Neutral'. In November 2021, this report was endorsed in full by ECC and the Climate Action Plan was produced to help deliver the recommendations set out in the 'Net Zero: Making Essex Carbon Neutral' report. The Climate Action Plan was updated in 2023 and is currently being updated again, for adoption later in 2025. The purpose of the ECAC to May 2025 was to enable and support implementation of the recommendations on the race to net zero across greater Essex by: 
• Advising on priorities for direction in the focus areas of the Special Interest Groups; 
• Reviewing and evaluating progress towards Net Zero; 
• Supporting the adoption of climate positive policies and practices at all levels of local authorities in the region; 
• Facilitating collective action across the county; 
• Encouraging public engagement with residents, businesses, schools, communities, third sector organisations and other institutions of Essex. The ECAC published the evaluation report 'The Essex Climate Action Commission (ECAC): Review of Impacts 2020-2024' in 2024. (https://www.essexclimate.org.uk/sites/default/files/Essex%20Climate%20Action%20Commission%20Impacts%202020-2024%20FINAL.pdf) In June 2025, it was confirmed by ECC that the lifetime of the ECAC would be extended to March 2027, allowing the ECAC to continue its work. There isn't an estimated total cost of implementation for this climate plan since it defines recommendations and targets for Essex wide partners and ECC who are responsible for its implementation where appropriate. (https://www.essexclimate.org.uk/).

Singapore Green Plan 2030 (SGP30) - Launched in Feb 2021, the Singapore Green Plan 2030 is a whole-of-nation movement to rally bold and collective action to tackle climate change. The Green Plan charts ambitious and concrete targets over the next 10 years, strengthening Singapore's commitments under the UN's 2030 Sustainable Development Agenda and Paris Agreement, and positioning Singapore to achieve its long-term net-zero emissions aspiration by or around mid-century. The Green Plan builds on the sustainability efforts of preceding decades, and will strengthen Singapore's economic, climate and resource resilience, improve the living environment of Singaporeans, and bring new business and job opportunities.The Green Plan has five key pillars:
a) City in Nature: to create a green, liveable and sustainable home for Singaporeans;
b) Sustainable Living: to make reducing carbon emissions, keeping our environment clean, and saving resources and energy a way of life in Singapore;
c) Energy Reset: to use cleaner energy and increase our energy efficiency to lower our carbon footprint; 
d) Green Economy: to seek green growth opportunities to create new jobs, transform our industries, and harness sustainability as a competitive advantage; and
e) Resilient Future: to build up Singapore's climate resilience and enhance our food security. 
The Green Plan is a living plan, which will evolve as we develop and refine our strategies, taking into account technological developments, and partner citizens, communities and businesses from all sectors to co-create solutions for sustainability.

Fulfilling the commitments undertaken by Lisbon for climate neutrality by 2030, with a direct impact at national and international level, constitutes a quite ambitious strategy not only for the City, but also for the organization itself and its employees, all its inhabitants, workers, students, and also the visitors who seek it. By taking on these commitments, Lisbon is taking co-responsibility in meeting the decarbonization and climate change adaptation goals, which impose: 
o	Bringing forward to 2030 actions and measures defined by the 2030 PAC for 2050;
o	Implementing additional actions and measures to reinforce mitigation, adaptation and transversal interventions in response to current and future challenges;
o	Rethinking the municipal budget, prioritizing planned actions and monitoring the respective implementation stages;
o	Continuing with the integration of financing from public (own revenues, subsidies and contributions), EU, and private (including loans) sources into the multi-annual investment plan for the coming years;
o	Prioritizing structuring projects, based on integrated, scalable and replicable actions, with scrupulously monitored schedules to allow for their timely completion;
o	Pursuing the Lisbon Participates strategy, with specific, inclusive, innovative, diversified actions, allowing the involvement of citizens, Businesses, universities and other key actors in the construction of the Lisbon ecosystem;
o	Continuing with the commitment aimed at Businesses and organizations, a strategy of joint mobilization in decision-making regarding the prioritization of investments and interventions to be followed;
o	Continuing with the strategy of individual and social accountability;
o	Making the Lisbon City Council more sustainable, resilient, a driver of change, and an accelerator of leadership.

Main activities are related to:
- development of the district heating network;

- use of waste and wastewater for energy production;

- afforestation, tree planting, and creation of green areas;

- building energy retrofitting;

- increase in the share of renewable energy sources (RES);

- industrial transformation;

- innovation and educational activities;

- increase in the share of renewable-based transport;

- development of recycling.

column23:
Para a cenarização climática procedeu-se à recolha e tratamento de informação climática futura (projeções) com recurso a diferentes modelos e para diferentes cenários climáticos (RCP 4.5 e 8.5), servindo como apoio para a identificação das possíveis alterações no clima futuro. As projeções climáticas utilizam cenários de emissões de GEE como dados de entrada (inputs) nos modelos climáticos, designados por Representative Concentration Pathways (RCP) ou Trajetórias Representativas de Concentrações (IPCC, 2013). Estes cenários representam emissões esperadas de GEE em função de diferentes evoluções futuras do desenvolvimento socioeconómico global. Sendo a concentração atual de CO2 400 ppm (partes por milhão), no presente estudo foram considerados dois cenários:
•	RCP 4.5 - que pressupõe uma trajetória de aumento da concentração de CO2 atmosférico até 520 ppm em 2070, com incremento menor até 2100;
•	RCP 8.5 - que pressupõe uma trajetória semelhante ao cenário RCP 4.5 até 2050, mas com aumento intensificado depois, atingindo uma concentração de CO2 de 950 ppm em 2100.

